    "href": "/api/guides/limits-and-compatibility",
    "searchText": "limits and compatibility design proctor clients for bounded inputs, rate controls, additive api evolution, and safe observability. limits and compatibility proctor rejects input before unbounded parsing. treat every request body, collection page, upload, log entry, and retry loop as bounded even when an installation has not published a narrower local policy. client limits - use the endpoint's documented limit range; never request or assume an unbounded collection. - stream file content where the client library permits it and enforce the endpoint's maximum before upload. - put only documented fields in request objects. unknown json fields are rejected. - send exactly one json value and the declared media type. - apply exponential backoff with jitter to documented transient failures and realtime reconnects. - respect an installation's response headers and stable error codes when it applies rate or concurrency controls. do not evade them with parallel credentials. the public multipart hard boundary is 100 mib plus 64 kib of overhead, but a specific route or installation policy can be lower. no generic maximum should override the endpoint schema. compatibility within /api/v1 the v1 contract can add operations, optional request fields, response fields, enum values where openness is documented, and new stable error codes. a robust client therefore: - ignores unknown response object fields; - handles unknown error and realtime event types safely; - does not exhaustively switch over an open value without a fallback; - sends only fields supported by the contract version it targets; and - does not derive meaning from opaque identifiers, cursors, etags, or tokens. removing a field, making an input required, or changing established behavior requires a new api version. the checked-in openapi contract is authoritative for a release; pin or archive the contract used to generate a client so a future additive update is a deliberate upgrade. observability without exposure safe client telemetry includes the http method, route template, status, duration, stable code , and request id . do not log bearer or refresh tokens, cookies, csrf values, invitation secrets, attempt credentials, student answers, raw exam content, or unbounded user-controlled fields."
